 succeeded in 0ms:
snp3.4:37.4 | S: and in this way your religious donation will succeed.” | T: In this way the offerings will succeed.”
snp3.4:38.1 | S: “The Buddha is worthy of the sacrificial cake, | T: Sundarika: “Master, the Awakened One,
snp3.4:38.2 | S: he is the supreme field of merit, | T: field of merit unexcelled in all the world,
snp3.4:38.3 | S: recipient of gifts from the whole world; | T: recipient for all the world deserves the sacrificial cake.
snp3.4:38.4 | S: what’s given to the worthy one is very fruitful.” | T: A gift given to you, master, bears great fruit.
snp3.4:39.1 | S: Then the brahmin Bhāradvāja of Sundarikā said to the Buddha, | T: Then the brahman Sundarika Bhāradvāja said to the Blessed One,
snp3.4:39.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent!
snp3.4:39.3 | S: As if he were righting the overturned, or revealing the hidden, or pointing out the path to the lost, or lighting a lamp in the dark so people with clear eyes can see what’s there, worthy Gotama has made the teaching clear in many ways. | T: Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
snp3.4:39.4 | S: I go for refuge to the worthy Gotama, to the teaching, and to the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
snp3.4:39.5 | S: May I receive the going forth, the ordination in the worthy Gotama’s presence?” | T: Let me obtain the Going-forth in Master Gotama’s presence, let me obtain Acceptance (into the Bhikkhu Saṅgha).”
snp3.4:39.6 | S: And the brahmin Bhāradvāja of Sundarikā received the going forth, the ordination in the Buddha’s presence. | T: Then the brahman Sundarika Bhāradvāja obtained the Going-forth in the Blessed One’s presence, he obtained Acceptance. And not long after his Acceptance—dwelling alone, secluded, heedful, ardent, & resolute—he in no long time reached & remained in the supreme goal of the holy life, for which clansmen rightly go forth from home into homelessness, knowing & realizing it for himself in the here-&-now. He knew: “Birth is ended, the holy life fulfilled, the task done. There is nothing further for the sake of this world.”
snp3.4:39.7 | S: And soon after, he became one of the perfected. | T: And so Ven. Sundarika Bhāradvāja became another one of the arahants.
snp3.4:39.8 | P: Sundarikabhāradvājasuttaṁ catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.3
snp4.3:1.1 | S: Some speak with malicious intent, | T: There are some who dispute corrupted at heart,
snp4.3:1.2 | S: while others speak set on truth. | T: and those who dispute their hearts set on truth,
snp4.3:1.3 | S: When disputes come up <j>a sage does not get involved, | T: but a sage doesn’t enter a dispute that’s arisen,
snp4.3:1.4 | S: which is why they’ve no barrenness at all. | T: which is why he has no rigidity anywhere at all.
snp4.3:2.1 | S: How can you transcend your own view | T: Now, how would one
snp4.3:2.2 | S: when you’re led by preference, dogmatic in belief? | T: led on by desire, entrenched in his likes,
snp4.3:2.3 | S: Inventing your own undertakings, | T: forming his own conclusions, overcome his own views?
snp4.3:2.4 | S: you’d speak according to your notion. | T: He’d dispute in line with the way that he knows.
snp4.3:3.1 | S: Some personages, unasked, tell others | T: Whoever boasts to others, unasked,
snp4.3:3.2 | S: of their own precepts and vows. | T: of his practices, habits,
snp4.3:3.3 | S: They have an ignoble nature, <j>say those who are skilled, | T: is, say the skilled, ignoble by nature—
snp4.3:3.4 | S: since they speak on themselves of their own accord. | T: he who speaks of himself of his own accord.
snp4.3:4.1 | S: A mendicant, peaceful, quenched, | T: But a monk at peace, fully unbound in himself,
snp4.3:4.2 | S: never boasts “thus am I” of their precepts. | T: not boasting of his habits —”That’s how I am”—
snp4.3:4.3 | S: They have a noble nature, say those who are skilled, | T: he, say the skilled, is noble by nature—
snp4.3:4.4 | S: who have no pretensions <j>about anything in the world. | T: he with no vanity anywhere in the world.
snp4.3:5.1 | S: For one who formulates and creates teachings, | T: One whose doctrines aren’t clean— fabricated, formed,
snp4.3:5.2 | S: and promotes them despite their defects, | T: given preference
snp4.3:5.3 | S: if they see an advantage for themselves, | T: when he sees it to his own advantage—
snp4.3:5.4 | S: they become dependent on that, <j>relying on unstable peace. | T: relies on a peace dependent on the provoked.
snp4.3:6.1 | S: It’s not easy to get over dogmatic views | T: Because entrenchments in views aren’t easily overcome
snp4.3:6.2 | S: adopted after judging among the teachings. | T: when considering what’s grasped among doctrines,
snp4.3:6.3 | S: That’s why, among all these dogmas, a person | T: that’s why a person embraces or rejects a doctrine— in light of these very entrenchments.
snp4.3:6.4 | S: ditches one teaching and takes up another. | T: (none)
snp4.3:7.1 | S: The cleansed one has no formulated view | T: Now, one who is cleansed has no theorized view
snp4.3:7.2 | S: at all in the world about the different realms. | T: about states of becoming or not- anywhere in the world.
snp4.3:7.3 | S: Having given up illusion and conceit, | T: Having abandoned conceit & illusion,
snp4.3:7.4 | S: by what path would they go? They are not involved. | T: by what means would he go? He isn’t involved,
snp4.3:8.1 | S: For one who gets involved <j>is embroiled in disputes about teachings— | T: for one who’s involved enters into disputes over doctrines.
snp4.3:8.2 | S: but how to dispute with the uninvolved? <j>About what? | T: But how—in connection with what— would you argue with one uninvolved?
snp4.3:8.3 | S: For picking up and putting down <j>is not what they do; | T: He has nothing embraced or rejected,
snp4.3:8.4 | S: they have shaken off all views in this very life. | T: has sloughed off every view right here—every one.
snp4.3:8.5 | P: Duṭṭhaṭṭhakasuttaṁ tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp4.5
snp4.5:1.1 | S: If, maintaining that theirs is the “ultimate” view, | T: When dwelling on views as “supreme,”
snp4.5:1.2 | S: a personage makes it out to be highest in the world; | T: a person makes them the utmost thing in the world,
snp4.5:1.3 | S: then they declare all others are “lesser”; | T: &, from that, calls all others inferior
snp4.5:1.4 | S: that’s why they’re not beyond disputes. | T: and so he’s not gone beyond disputes.
snp4.5:2.1 | S: If they see an advantage for themselves | T: When he sees his own advantage
snp4.5:2.2 | S: in what’s seen, heard, or thought; <j>or in precepts or vows, | T: in what’s seen, heard, sensed, or in habits & practices,
snp4.5:2.3 | S: in that case, having adopted that one alone, | T: seizing it there
snp4.5:2.4 | S: they see all others as inferior. | T: he sees all else, all others, as inferior.
snp4.5:3.1 | S: Those who are skilled say that, too, is a knot, | T: That, too, say the skilled, is a binding knot:
snp4.5:3.2 | S: relying on which people see others as lesser. | T: that in dependence on which you regard another as inferior.
snp4.5:3.3 | S: That’s why a mendicant ought not rely | T: So a monk shouldn’t be dependent
snp4.5:3.4 | S: on what’s seen, heard, or thought, <j>or on precepts and vows. | T: on what’s seen, heard, or sensed, or on habits & practices;
snp4.5:4.1 | S: Nor would they form a view about the world | T: nor should he theorize a view in the world
snp4.5:4.2 | S: through a notion or through precepts and vows. | T: in connection with knowledge or habits & practices;
snp4.5:4.3 | S: They would never represent themselves as “equal”, | T: shouldn’t take himself to be “equal”;
snp4.5:4.4 | S: nor conceive themselves “worse” or “better”. | T: shouldn’t think himself inferior or superlative.
snp4.5:5.1 | S: What was picked up has been set down <j>and is not grasped again; | T: Abandoning what he’d embraced, not clinging,
snp4.5:5.2 | S: they form no dependency even on notions. | T: he doesn’t make himself dependent even in connection with knowledge;
snp4.5:5.3 | S: They follow no side among the factions, | T: doesn’t follow a faction among those who are split;
snp4.5:5.4 | S: and believe in no view at all. | T: doesn’t fall back on any view whatsoever.
snp4.5:6.1 | S: One here who has no wish for either end— | T: One who isn’t inclined toward either side
snp4.5:6.2 | S: for any state of existence in this life or the next— | T: —becoming or not-, here or beyond—
snp4.5:6.3 | S: has adopted no dogma at all | T: who has no entrenchment
snp4.5:6.4 | S: after judging among the teachings. | T: when considering what’s grasped among doctrines,
snp4.5:7.1 | S: For them not even the tiniest idea is formulated here | T: hasn’t the least theorized perception
snp4.5:7.2 | S: regarding what is seen, heard, or thought. | T: with regard to what’s seen, heard, or sensed.
snp4.5:7.3 | S: That brahmin does not grasp any view— | T: By whom, with what, should he be pigeonholed here in the world? —this brahman who hasn’t adopted views.
snp4.5:7.4 | S: how could anyone in this world judge them? | T: (none)
snp4.5:8.1 | S: They don’t make things up or promote them, | T: They don’t theorize, don’t yearn,
snp4.5:8.2 | S: and don’t subscribe to any of the doctrines. | T: don’t adhere even to doctrines.
snp4.5:8.3 | S: The brahmin has no need to be led by precept or vow; | T: A brahman not led by habits or practices,
snp4.5:8.4 | S: gone to the far shore, one such does not return. | T: gone to the beyond —Such— doesn’t fall back.
snp4.5:8.5 | P: Paramaṭṭhakasuttaṁ pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn11.14
sn11.14:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn11.14:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants, | T: There he addressed the monks:
sn11.14:1.3 | S: “Mendicants!” | T: “Monks!”
sn11.14:1.4 | S: “Venerable sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
sn11.14:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said,
sn11.14:2.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, there was a poor person, destitute and pitiful. | T: “Monks, once there was a man living in this very Rājagaha—a poor, pitiful wretch of a person.
sn11.14:2.2 | S: They took up faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom in the teaching and training proclaimed by the Realized One. | T: He undertook conviction in the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Blessed One, undertook virtue, undertook learning, undertook relinquishment, undertook discernment.
sn11.14:2.3 | S: After undertaking these things, when their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm, in the company of the gods of the thirty-three. | T: He—having undertaken conviction in the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Blessed One, having undertaken virtue, having undertaken learning, having undertaken relinquishment, having undertaken discernment—on the breakup of the body, after death, reappeared in a good destination, a heavenly world, in the company of the devas of the Thirty-three.
sn11.14:2.4 | S: There they outshone the other gods in beauty and glory. | T: There he outshone the other devas in beauty & in rank.
sn11.14:2.5 | S: But the gods of the thirty-three complained, grumbled, and objected, | T: “Then the devas of the Thirty-three were indignant, annoyed, & complained:
sn11.14:2.6 | S: ‘Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: ‘Isn’t it amazing, good sirs! Isn’t it astounding!
sn11.14:2.7 | S: For when this godling was a human being in their past life they were poor, destitute, and pitiful. | T: Before, this young deva, when he was a human being, was a poor, pitiful wretch of a person.
sn11.14:2.8 | S: And when their body broke up, after death, they were reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm, in the company of the gods of the thirty-three. | T: But now, with the breakup of the body, after death, he has reappeared in a good destination, a heavenly world, in the company of the devas of the Thirty-three.
sn11.14:2.9 | S: Here they outshine the other gods in beauty and glory.’ | T: There he outshines the other devas in beauty & in rank.’
sn11.14:3.1 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, addressed the gods of the thirty-three, | T: “Then Sakka the deva-king addressed the devas of the Thirty-three,
sn11.14:3.2 | S: ‘Good fellows, don’t complain about this godling. | T: ‘Dear sirs, don’t be indignant with this young deva.
sn11.14:3.3 | S: When this godling was a human being in their past life they took up faith, ethics, learning, generosity, and wisdom in the teaching and training proclaimed by the Realized One. | T: Before, this young deva, when he was a human being, undertook conviction in the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Blessed One, undertook virtue, undertook learning, undertook relinquishment, undertook discernment.
sn11.14:3.4 | S: After undertaking these things, when their body broke up, after death, they’ve been reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm, in the company of the gods of the thirty-three. | T: He—having undertaken conviction in the Dhamma & Vinaya proclaimed by the Blessed One, having undertaken virtue, having undertaken learning, having undertaken relinquishment, having undertaken discernment—on the breakup of the body, after death, has reappeared in a good destination, a heavenly world, in the company of the devas of the Thirty-three.
sn11.14:3.5 | S: Here they outshine the other gods in beauty and glory.’ | T: There he outshines the other devas in beauty & in rank.’
sn11.14:3.6 | S: Then Sakka, lord of gods, guiding the gods of the thirty-three, spoke this verse: | T: “Then, conciliating the devas of the Thirty-three, Sakka the deva-king on that occasion recited these verses:
sn11.14:4.1 | S: ‘Whoever has faith in the Realized One, | T: ‘One whose conviction in the Tathāgata
sn11.14:4.2 | S: unwavering and well grounded; | T: is well-established, unshakable;
sn11.14:4.3 | S: whose ethical conduct is good, | T: whose virtue is admirable,
sn11.14:4.4 | S: praised and loved by the noble ones; | T: appealing to the noble ones, praised;
sn11.14:5.1 | S: who has confidence in the Saṅgha, | T: who has confidence in the Saṅgha,
sn11.14:5.2 | S: and correct view: | T: & vision made straight:
sn11.14:5.3 | S: they’re said to be prosperous, | T: “Not poor,” they say of him.
sn11.14:5.4 | S: their life is not in vain. | T: Not in vain his life.
sn11.14:6.1 | S: So let the wise devote themselves | T: (none)
sn11.14:6.2 | S: to faith, ethical behaviour, | T: So conviction & virtue,
sn11.14:6.3 | S: confidence, and insight into the teaching, | T: confidence & Dhamma-vision should be cultivated by the intelligent,
sn11.14:6.4 | S: remembering the instructions of the Buddhas.’” | T: remembering the Buddhas’ teachings.’”

## an3.40
an3.40:1.1 | S: “There are, mendicants, these three things to put in charge. | T: “There are these three governing principles.
an3.40:1.2 | S: What three? | T: Which three?
an3.40:1.3 | S: Putting oneself, the world, or the teaching in charge. | T: The self as a governing principle, the cosmos as a governing principle, and the Dhamma as a governing principle.
an3.40:1.4 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting oneself in charge? | T: “And what is the self as a governing principle?
an3.40:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: There is the case where a monk, having gone to a wilderness, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty dwelling, reflects on this:
an3.40:1.6 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘It is not for the sake of robes that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness;
an3.40:1.7 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: it is not for the sake of almsfood, for the sake of lodgings, or for the sake of this or that state of (future) becoming that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness.
an3.40:1.8 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death; by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: Simply that I am beset by birth, aging, & death; by sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs; beset by stress, overcome with stress,
an3.40:1.9 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: (and I hope,) “Perhaps the end of this entire mass of suffering & stress might be known!”
an3.40:1.10 | S: But it would not be appropriate for me to seek sensual pleasures like those I abandoned when I went forth, or even worse.’ | T: Now, if I were to seek the same sort of sensual pleasures that I abandoned in going forth from home into homelessness—or a worse sort—that would not be fitting for me.’
an3.40:1.11 | S: Then they reflect: | T: So he reflects on this:
an3.40:1.12 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘My persistence will be aroused & not lax; my mindfulness established & not confused; my body calm & not aroused; my mind centered & unified.’
an3.40:1.13 | S: Putting themselves in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having made himself his governing principle, he abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful, abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is unblameworthy, and looks after himself in a pure way.
an3.40:1.14 | S: This is called putting oneself in charge. | T: This is called the self as a governing principle.
an3.40:2.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting the world in charge? | T: “And what is the cosmos as a governing principle?
an3.40:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: There is the case where a monk, having gone to a wilderness, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty dwelling, reflects on this:
an3.40:2.3 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘It’s not for the sake of robes that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness;
an3.40:2.4 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: it’s not for the sake of almsfood, for the sake of lodgings, or for the sake of this or that state of (future) becoming that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness.
an3.40:2.5 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death, by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: Simply that I am beset by birth, aging, & death; by sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs; beset by stress, overcome with stress,
an3.40:2.6 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: (and I hope,) “Perhaps the end of this entire mass of suffering & stress might be known!”
an3.40:2.7 | S: And now, since I’ve now gone forth, I might have sensual, malicious, or cruel thoughts. But the population of the world is large, | T: Now if I, having gone forth, were to think thoughts of sensuality, thoughts of ill will, or thoughts of harmfulness: Great is the community of this cosmos,
an3.40:2.8 | S: and there are ascetics and brahmins who have psychic power—they’re clairvoyant, and can read the minds of others. | T: and in the great community of this cosmos there are contemplatives & brahmans endowed with psychic power, clairvoyant, skilled (in reading) the minds of others.
an3.40:2.9 | S: They see far without being seen, even by those close; and they understand the minds of others. | T: They can see even from afar. Even up close, they are invisible. With their awareness they know the minds of others.
an3.40:2.10 | S: They would know me: | T: They would know this of me:
an3.40:2.11 | S: “Look at this gentleman; he’s gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, but he’s living mixed up with bad, unskillful qualities.” | T: “Look, my friends, at this clansman who—though he has in good faith gone forth from the home life into homelessness—remains overcome with evil, unskillful mental qualities.”
an3.40:2.12 | S: And there are deities, too, who have psychic power—they’re clairvoyant, and can read the minds of others. | T: There are also devas endowed with psychic power, clairvoyant, skilled (in reading) the minds of others.
an3.40:2.13 | S: They see far without being seen, even by those close; and they understand the minds of others. | T: They can see even from afar. Even up close, they are invisible. With their awareness they know the minds of others.
an3.40:2.14 | S: They would know me: | T: They would know this of me:
an3.40:2.15 | S: “Look at this gentleman; he’s gone forth out of faith from the lay life to homelessness, but he’s living mixed up with bad, unskillful qualities.”’ | T: “Look, my friends, at this clansman who—though he has in good faith gone forth from the home life into homelessness—remains overcome with evil, unskillful mental qualities.”’
an3.40:2.16 | S: Then they reflect: | T: So he reflects on this:
an3.40:2.17 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘My persistence will be aroused & not lax; my mindfulness established & not confused; my body calm & not aroused; my mind centered & unified.’
an3.40:2.18 | S: Putting the world in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having made the cosmos his governing principle, he abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful, abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is unblameworthy, and looks after himself in a pure way.
an3.40:2.19 | S: This is called putting the world in charge. | T: This is called the cosmos as a governing principle.
an3.40:3.1 | S: And what, mendicants, is putting the teaching in charge? | T: “And what is the Dhamma as a governing principle?
an3.40:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has gone to a wilderness, or to the root of a tree, or to an empty hut, and reflects like this: | T: There is the case where a monk, having gone to a wilderness, to the foot of a tree, or to an empty dwelling, reflects on this:
an3.40:3.3 | S: ‘I didn’t go forth from the lay life to homelessness for the sake of a robe, | T: ‘It’s not for the sake of robes that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness;
an3.40:3.4 | S: almsfood, lodgings, or rebirth in this or that state. | T: it’s not for the sake of almsfood, for the sake of lodgings, or for the sake of this or that state of (future) becoming that I have gone forth from the home life into homelessness.
an3.40:3.5 | S: But I was swamped by rebirth, old age, and death, by sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. I was swamped by suffering, mired in suffering. | T: Simply that I am beset by birth, aging, & death; by sorrows, lamentations, pains, distresses, & despairs; beset by stress, overcome with stress,
an3.40:3.6 | S: And I thought, “Hopefully I can make an end to this entire mass of suffering.” | T: (and I hope,) “Perhaps the end of this entire mass of suffering & stress might be known!”
an3.40:3.7 | S: The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves. | T: Now, the Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.
an3.40:3.8 | S: I have spiritual companions who live knowing and seeing. | T: There are companions in the holy life who dwell knowing & seeing it.
an3.40:3.9 | S: Now that I’ve gone forth in this well explained teaching and training, it would not be appropriate for me to live lazy and heedless.’ | T: If I—having gone forth in this well-taught Dhamma & Vinaya—were to remain lazy & heedless, that would not be fitting for me.’
an3.40:3.10 | S: Then they reflect: | T: So he reflects on this:
an3.40:3.11 | S: ‘My energy shall be roused up and unflagging, mindfulness shall be established and lucid, my body shall be tranquil and undisturbed, and my mind shall be immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘My persistence will be aroused & not lax; my mindfulness established & not confused; my body calm & not aroused; my mind centered & unified.’
an3.40:3.12 | S: Putting the teaching in charge, they give up the unskillful and develop the skillful, they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, and they keep themselves pure. | T: Having made the Dhamma his governing principle, he abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful, abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is unblameworthy, and looks after himself in a pure way.
an3.40:3.13 | S: This is called putting the teaching in charge. | T: This is called the Dhamma as a governing principle.
an3.40:3.14 | S: These are the three things to put in charge. | T: “These are the three governing principles.”
an3.40:4.1 | S: There’s no privacy in the world, | T: There is in the cosmos no secret place
an3.40:4.2 | S: for someone who does bad deeds. | T: for one who has done an evil deed.
an3.40:4.3 | S: You’ll know for yourself, | T: Your own self knows, my good man,
an3.40:4.4 | S: whether you’ve lied or told the truth. | T: whether you are true or false.
an3.40:5.1 | S: When you witness your good self, | T: You underestimate the fine witness that is yourself,
an3.40:5.2 | S: you despise it; | T: (none)
an3.40:5.3 | S: while you disguise | T: you with evil in yourself that then you hide.
an3.40:5.4 | S: your bad self inside yourself. | T: (none)
an3.40:6.1 | S: The gods and the Realized One see | T: The devas & Tathāgatas see
an3.40:6.2 | S: the fool who lives unjustly in the world. | T: the fool who goes about off-pitch in the cosmos.
an3.40:6.3 | S: So with yourself in charge, live mindfully; | T: Thus you should go about self-governed, mindful;
an3.40:6.4 | S: with the world in charge, <j>be alert and practice absorption; | T: governed by the cosmos, masterful, absorbed in jhāna;
an3.40:6.5 | S: with the teaching in charge, <j>live in line with that teaching: | T: governed by the Dhamma, acting in line with the Dhamma.
an3.40:6.6 | S: a sage who tries for the truth doesn’t deteriorate. | T: The sage who makes an effort in truth doesn’t fall back.

