## ud3.7
ud3.7:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud3.7:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha at the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
ud3.7:1.3 | S: Now at that time Venerable Mahākassapa was staying in the Pipphali cave. Having entered a certain state of immersion, he sat cross-legged for seven days without moving. | T: And on that occasion Ven. Mahā Kassapa was staying at the Pipphali Cave, sitting for seven days in a single session, having attained a certain level of concentration.
ud3.7:1.4 | S: When seven days had passed, Mahākassapa emerged from that state of immersion. | T: Then, with the passing of seven days, he emerged from that concentration.
ud3.7:1.5 | S: It occurred to him, | T: To him, emerging from that concentration, the thought occurred:
ud3.7:1.6 | S: “Why not enter Rājagaha for almsfood?” | T: “What if I were to go into Rājagaha for alms?”
ud3.7:2.1 | S: Now at that time five hundred deities were ready and eager for the chance to offer alms to Mahākassapa. | T: Now on that occasion 500 devatās were in a state of eagerness for the chance to give alms to Ven. Mahā Kassapa.
ud3.7:2.2 | S: But Mahākasspa turned down those deities. In the morning, he robed up, took his bowl and robe, and entered Rājagaha for alms. | T: But Ven. Mahā Kassapa, turning down those 500 devatās, early in the morning adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went into Rājagaha for alms.
ud3.7:2.3 | S: Now at that time Sakka, lord of gods, wished to give alms to Mahākassapa. | T: Now on that occasion Sakka, the deva-king, wanted to give alms to Ven. Mahā Kassapa.
ud3.7:3.2 | S: Having manifested in the appearance of an embroiderer, he wove the thread. The titan maiden Sujā spun it onto the spindle. | T: So, assuming the appearance of a weaver, he was working a loom, while Sujātā, an asura-maiden, filled the shuttle.
ud3.7:3.3 | S: Then, as Mahākassapa wandered indiscriminately for almsfood in Rājagaha, he approached Sakka’s house. | T: Then, as Ven. Mahā Kassapa was going on an almsround that bypassed no donors in Rājagaha, he arrived at Sakka’s home.
ud3.7:3.4 | S: Seeing Mahākassapa coming off in the distance, | T: Sakka saw him coming from afar and, on seeing him,
ud3.7:3.5 | S: Sakka came out of his house, greeted him, and took the bowl from his hand. He re-entered the house and filled the bowl with rice from the pot. | T: came out of house to meet him. Taking the bowl from his hand, entered the house, took cooked rice from the pot, filled the bowl, and gave it back to Ven. Mahā Kassapa.
ud3.7:3.6 | S: That almsfood had many tasty soups and sauces. | T: And that gift of alms included many kinds of curry, many kinds of sauces.
ud3.7:3.7 | S: Then it occurred to Mahākassapa, | T: The thought occurred to Ven. Mahā Kassapa,
ud3.7:3.8 | S: “Now, what being is this who has such psychic power?” | T: “Now, who is this being with such power & might as this?”
ud3.7:3.9 | S: It occurred to him, | T: Then the thought occurred to him,
ud3.7:3.10 | S: “This is Sakka, lord of gods.” | T: “This is Sakka, the deva-king, isn’t it?”
ud3.7:3.11 | S: Knowing this, he said to Sakka, | T: On realizing this, he said to Sakka,
ud3.7:3.12 | S: “This is your doing, Kosiya; | T: “Is this your doing, Kosiya?
ud3.7:3.13 | S: don’t do such a thing again.” | T: Don’t ever do anything like this again.”
ud3.7:3.14 | S: “But Honorable Kassapa, we too need merit! | T: “We, too, need merit, Ven. Kassapa. We, too, have use for merit.”
ud3.7:3.15 | S: We too ought to make merit.” | T: (none)
ud3.7:4.1 | S: Then Sakka bowed and respectfully circled Mahākassapa, keeping him on his right. Then he rose into the air and, sitting cross-legged in midair, expressed this heartfelt sentiment three times: | T: Then, bowing down to Ven. Mahā Kassapa and circling him to the right, Sakka rose up into the air and, while up in the sky, exclaimed three times:
ud3.7:4.2 | S: “Oh the gift, the best gift is well established in Kassapa! | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:4.3 | S: Oh the gift, the best gift is well established in Kassapa! | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:4.4 | S: Oh the gift, the best gift is well established in Kassapa!” | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:4.5 | S: With clairaudience that is purified and superhuman, the Buddha heard Sakka express this heartfelt sentiment while sitting in the sky. | T: The Blessed One–with his divine hearing-property, surpassing that of the human–heard Sakka the deva-king, while up in the sky, exclaiming three times:
ud3.7:4.6 | P: “aho dānaṁ paramadānaṁ kassape suppatiṭṭhitaṁ. | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:4.7 | P: Aho dānaṁ paramadānaṁ kassape suppatiṭṭhitaṁ. | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:4.8 | P: Aho dānaṁ paramadānaṁ kassape suppatiṭṭhitan”ti. | T: “O the alms, the foremost alms, well-established in Kassapa!”
ud3.7:5.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: On realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud3.7:6.1 | S: “A mendicant who relies on alms, | T: The monk going for alms,
ud3.7:6.2 | S: self-supported, providing for no other; | T: supporting himself and no other:
ud3.7:6.3 | S: the unaffected one is envied by even the gods, | T: The devas adore one who is Such,
ud3.7:6.4 | S: calm and ever mindful.” | T: calmed & ever mindful.
ud3.7:6.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)


